Think of Docker containers like people living in housing setups:

---

### The Default Bridge = A Busy Public Train Station

* When you don't create a network, Docker drops all containers into one big, shared default area (the default bridge).
* In this crowded station, there is **no name directory or receptionist**.
* If you want to talk to someone, you cannot just shout their name (`db`) because nobody is keeping track of who is who.
* The only way to reach someone is if you already know their exact, temporary **phone number (IP address)**.

---

### The Custom Bridge (`app-net`) = A Private Office Team with an Internal Phone Directory

* When you create your own network (`docker network create app-net`), Docker sets up a dedicated, private workspace for those containers.


* More importantly, Docker automatically assigns a **built-in digital directory / receptionist (an embedded DNS server)** to that private room.


* Whenever a container joins this network with a name (like `--name db`), Docker automatically writes down its name and IP address in this internal phonebook.


* When another container inside that room says, *"Hey, connect me to `db`"*, Docker looks up the directory, finds the exact IP address instantly, and connects them.



---

### Why doesn't Docker do this on the default bridge too?

* **Legacy reasons:** The default bridge is old Docker behavior. Long ago, Docker used rigid, manual config files (`/etc/hosts`) and `--link` flags to connect containers. Changing how the default network works now would break millions of older legacy apps that rely on that old behavior.
* **Isolation & Security:** By not auto-connecting everything by name on the default network, Docker forces you to deliberately group your containers into their own private networks so unrelated apps cannot easily discover and snoop on each other.

